    "title": "Why Your Job Applications Get Ignored (Free Book)",
    "status": "published",
    "scheduled_at": "2026-11-04T10:04:58Z",
    "parameters_json": "{\"annotation_density\": 3, \"avatar_frequency\": 2, \"brand_preset\": \"golide-editorial\", \"editorial_manifest\": \"beat-motion-audio-v1\", \"image_change_bias\": 4, \"motion_intensity\": 4, \"pacing\": \"fast\", \"schedule_rule\": \"studio-external-video\", \"sound_design\": 3, \"visual_density\": 4, \"voice\": \"owner-recording-only\"}"
  },
  "narration": "You know what's frustrating?\nSending out 50 job applications in a month\nand not hearing back from a single one.\nAnd honestly, sometimes it's not even about how many jobs you're applying for.\nIt's *how* you're applying.\nThink about it.\nYou're taking the same CV, sending it to 50 different companies,\nand hoping someone notices you.\nBut recruiters can tell when an application hasn't really been tailored to the role.\nSo here's what I'd do differently.\nFirst, stop applying for every job you come across.\nFocus on the ones where you actually have a chance.\nBefore applying, understand what the employer is looking for.\nThen make sure your CV shows that fit right from the beginning,\nwith actual evidence of what you can do.\nAnd don't just send the application and forget about it.\nKeep track of where you've applied and follow up.\nBecause you'd be surprised how many people never do that.\nThe idea is simple: fewer applications, but better chances of getting interviews.\nI've actually put this entire approach into a\nshort book called **The Job Search Conversion System**.\nIt's a 14-day system, and the Kindle version is currently free until Thursday.\nYou don't even need a Kindle.\nYou can read it using the free Kindle app on your phone.\nI've left the link in the description.\nGo grab your copy while it's still free.\n",
